## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about multi-word bit manipulations and safe 64→128-bit extension without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit reversal via two 64-bit reversals and swapping halves
- Parallel-bits reversal (mask/shifts)
- Endianness-insensitive mapping of bit indices

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(x) {
  x = (x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n | (x & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n;
  x = (x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n | (x & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n;
  x = (x >> 4n) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n;
  x = (x >> 8n) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n;
  x = (x >> 16n) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n;
  x = (x >> 32n) | (x << 32n);
  return x;
}
function reverse128(hi, lo) {
  const revLo = reverse64(lo);
  const revHi = reverse64(hi);
  return { hi: revLo, lo: revHi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to streaming 128-bit values?
- How to verify correctness with random tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Transposition in bit-sliced form is a classic challenge that tests mastery of layout tricks and how data moves across a 128-bit container without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice layout and Morton-order ideas
- Multi-stage bit redistribution with masks
- Branchless, vector-style bit manipulation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation outline for guidance
uint128 transpose8x16(uint128 x) {
  // stage 1: swap adjacent bits
  x = (x & 0x5555...5555) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555...5555);
  // stage 2: swap pairs
  x = (x & 0x3333...3333) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333...3333);
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x & 0x0F0F...0F0F) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F...0F0F);
  // stage 4: move bytes
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 64-bit inputs without 128-bit math?
- How to test exhaustively across 32-bit space?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply known bit-hack patterns to solve a common problem without loops or libs. Shows understanding of data-parallel aggregation and portability across compilers.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) counting
- Bit masks and shifts to sum bits in chunks
- Portability across 32-bit integers

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t countBits32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 64-bit integers?
- What are alternative methods and trade-offs (lookup-table vs SWAR)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-twiddling without branches, a staple in low-level C/C++ interviews.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Constant-time bit tricks without loops
- Small lookup for nibble parity

## Code Example
```c
uint32 parity32(uint32 x){
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x &= 0x0F;
  return (0x6996 >> x) & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle 64-bit inputs?  
- What are pitfalls with compiler optimizations here?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to accumulate using wider accumulators? 
- What is the performance impact on platforms with native popcount intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes fluency with SWAR bit-twiddling and 128-bit composition, ensuring branchless correctness.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit SWAR reversal
- 128-bit result from {hi: rev64(lo), lo: rev64(hi)}
- Mask/shift sequences to flip bit blocks without branches

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t rev64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) | (x << 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = rev64(x.lo);
  r.lo = rev64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge alignment and performance implications?
- Could this approach be generalized to arbitrary widths with minimal changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests fundamental bitwise thinking and the ability to apply a classic parity reduction without branches. It mirrors low-level optimizations used in data integrity checks.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-folding for parity
- Right-shifts for subtraction-free folding
- Constant-time bit-twiddling

## Code Example

```javascript
// (Note: actual signature shown in question; C-like syntax used for clarity)
uint8_t parity32(uint32_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  return (uint8_t)((x & 0xF) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs? 
- What are potential pitfalls with compiler optimizations on such patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement without 128-bit type support?
- What are performance trade-offs for different compilers?
